response:
The Apache Camel's MIME-Multipart data format automatically apply Base64 encoding to all parts of the multipart message, including attachments. This is most likely what is causing your Signature file to be encoded incorrectly. 

If for some reason you need to attach a part that should not be Base64-encoded, or encoded in a different way, you'll need to construct the MIME-Multipart structure yourself, without using builtin marshal feature.

Here's an example using javax.mail.internet.MimeMultipart and javax.mail.internet.MimeBodyPart:

```java
public void attachSignature(Exchange exchange) throws Exception {
    MimeMultipart multiPart = new MimeMultipart();

    AttachmentMessage in = exchange.getIn(AttachmentMessage.class);
    String body = in.getBody().toString();

    // Add body part
    MimeBodyPart mimeBodyPart = new MimeBodyPart();
    mimeBodyPart.setText(body, "UTF-8", "plain");
    multiPart.addBodyPart(mimeBodyPart);

    // Add signature part
    String signature = messageSignature.generateSignature(body);
    byte[] byteSignature = signature.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
    DataSource dataSource = new ByteArrayDataSource(byteSignature, "application/octet-stream");
    MimeBodyPart signaturePart = new MimeBodyPart();
    signaturePart.setDataHandler(new DataHandler(dataSource));
    signaturePart.setFileName("Signature.sig");
    multiPart.addBodyPart(signaturePart);

    // Replace the original body with the multipart content
    in.setBody(multiPart.getContent(), multiPart.getContentType());
}

from(source)
    .bean(this, "populatedestinationDetails")
    .bean(this, "attachSignature")
    .to(destination);
```

